proposition.claim:
Theorem 1.9 allows the logarithmic first Chern class to be nef or anti-nef. Theorem 1.15 instead uses the log Calabi–Yau setup, the nonnegative boundary coefficients for the negative logarithmic first Chern class, and the subcomplex \(B\) of good rays. It does not replace \(B(\mathbb Z)\) with all of \(\Sigma(X)(\mathbb Z)\). The structure constants use a logarithmic point constraint and a negative output contact order; they are defined to vanish unless \(A\cdot c_1(\Theta_{X/k})=0\).

The principal checked dependency is: \[\text{Lemma 8.4(1)}
\longrightarrow \text{Theorem 8.12}
\longrightarrow \text{Theorem 4.28}
\longrightarrow \text{Theorem 4.4}
\longrightarrow \text{Theorems 1.9 and 1.15}.\] Theorem 8.12, Step 1 (p. 152), explicitly invokes Lemma 8.4(1). Theorem 4.4 on p. 83 combines the comparisons and independence of modulus. Other dependencies in that chain have only the extent of verification described below.

From P=Q+_R^fine Rlambda embedded in torsion-free Qgp, conclude Spec k[Psat] -> Spec k[P] is one-to-one on geometric points.
